 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the town law, in relation to the preservation of lands\nnecessary to protect fisheries and water dependent uses essential to maintain\nand enhance maritime heritage",
    "basePrintNo": "S6379",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6379-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §64-e, Town L",
    "lawSection": "Town Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6379\n\nSPONSOR: LAVALLE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the town law, in relation to the pres-\nervation of lands necessary to protect fisheries and water dependent\nuses essential to maintain and enhance maritime heritage\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis legislation would expand the definition of preservation of communi-\nty character under the Peconic Bay Community Preservation Fund to\ninclude projects that will help preserve and protect the maritime herit-\nage of Long Island's East End.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS: Section 1. Amends subdivision 4 of Section 64-e\nof the Town Law to include the preservation of lands necessary to\nprotect fisheries and water dependent uses essential to maintain and\nenhance maritime heritage as a means of preserving community character\nunder the Peconic Bay Community Preservation Fund (CPF).\n\nSection 2. Immediate effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe CPF is a public program managed by each of the five East End Towns\non Long Island (Southampton, East Hampton, Shelter Island, Riverhead and\nSouthold) for the protection of farmland, open space, and community\ncharacter. This program was overwhelmingly approved in a public referen-\ndum in 1998 and has since generated over $1.4 billion to protect over\n10,000 acres of land on the East End.  The program was amended in 2015,\nwhere 80% voters on the East End approved the extension the provisions\nof the CPF through the year 2050 and allowed up to 20% of the funds to\nbe utilized for water quality improvement projects. This legislation is\nnecessary to further expand upon the provisions of the CPF to help\npreserve and protect the lands surrounding the waters that are essential\nin keeping the historic fishing industry thriving and enhance resources\nthat are significant to the East End's waterfront economy.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019: New Legislation\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
